      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE AND DESIGN",
          "content": "Ebselen, an organoselenium compound is able to modulate the inflammatory response in rodents. In the present study, the anti-inflammatory and antinociceptive activity of diaryl diselenides and ebselen was studied."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS",
          "content": "Adult male Wistar rats and albino mice were treated with diaryl diselenides and ebselen in different doses."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Carrageenin-induced paw edema, tail-flick, formalin, acetic acid-induced abdominal writhing and capsaicin models of pain were carried out. Data were analyzed by ANOVA followed by Duncan's multiple range when appropriate."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In all models, the most promising profile was displayed by diphenyl diselenide, which produced anti-inflammatory and antinociceptive activity significantly higher than ebselen. Diphenyl diselenide also produced dose-dependent antinociception when assessed in acetic acid-induced abdominal constriction, tail-flick test or formalin and capsaicin-induced nociception."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The data presented here provide evidence that administration of diphenyl diselenide produced anti-inflammatory and antinociceptive activity."
        }
      ]},
